Some say it's just about ready to burst.

01:17:08.450 --> 01:17:09.710
How about another round?

01:17:11.080 --> 01:17:12.680
Theodore, what are you betting?

01:17:13.830 --> 01:17:14.350
This pin.

01:17:17.980 --> 01:17:18.960
All right, here goes.

01:17:19.280 --> 01:17:20.800
I'll beat him this time.

01:17:21.350 --> 01:17:23.070
And look at this.

01:17:23.460 --> 01:17:24.960
Steam locomotive rails.

01:17:25.120 --> 01:17:27.640
A steam engine used to run there back when I was a boy.

01:17:27.960 --> 01:17:29.960
Choo-choo-choo. Chugga-chugga-chugga-chugga-chugga.

01:17:30.090 --> 01:17:31.770
Choo choo chugga chugga, huh?

01:17:34.280 --> 01:17:36.480
These kids don't appreciate history.

01:17:41.890 --> 01:17:43.710
Give us the best thing you own.

01:17:43.730 --> 01:17:45.160
I don't anything like that.

01:17:45.700 --> 01:17:47.520
Sure you do, you know what it is.

01:17:47.640 --> 01:17:48.940
Give us your energy cassette.

01:17:51.970 --> 01:17:53.470
Come on, it's only a game.

01:17:53.730 --> 01:17:55.310
Yeah, come on, take it out.

01:17:55.630 --> 01:17:59.350
Yeah, hey, why not take a look inside his body?

01:18:00.000 --> 01:18:04.240
So this is how it looks inside.

01:18:04.600 --> 01:18:06.220
Hey, let me take a look, too.

01:18:06.460 --> 01:18:07.560
Let me see, too.

01:18:16.080 --> 01:18:18.920
Just what do you kids think you're doing?

01:18:19.220 --> 01:18:21.780
Nothing, just looking inside Astro's body.

01:18:28.220 --> 01:18:29.760
Is this an unmanned station?

01:18:30.000 --> 01:18:32.620
That's right. Only stations in large cities are mad.

01:18:32.820 --> 01:18:35.600
Daddy Walrus, what's the big thing over there?

01:18:35.880 --> 01:18:37.640
That's the old obsolete dam.

01:18:38.000 --> 01:18:40.240
That's in our tour schedule, isn't it?

01:18:40.440 --> 01:18:41.580
It certainly is.

01:18:41.900 --> 01:18:43.720
This is where we're staying tonight.

01:18:43.980 --> 01:18:45.940
Hey, over there.

01:18:46.140 --> 01:18:47.240
That's a Holstein.

01:18:50.100 --> 01:18:52.460
Thanks for offering to take care of us.

01:18:52.770 --> 01:18:53.450
My pleasure.

01:18:56.130 --> 01:18:57.600
A steam locomotive.

01:18:57.720 --> 01:18:58.400
Oh, yeah?

01:18:58.740 --> 01:18:59.980
I saw a steam locomotive.

01:19:01.840 --> 01:19:03.300
What's a steam locomotive?

01:19:03.600 --> 01:19:05.300
An old train that ran by steam.

01:19:05.780 --> 01:19:07.100
That's a pile of rusty metal

01:19:07.100 --> 01:19:08.280
if I ever saw one.

01:19:10.000 --> 01:19:10.980
What's the idea?

01:19:11.320 --> 01:19:13.920
That's for calling the D-51 bad names.

01:19:14.540 --> 01:19:15.920
Oh, yeah? Big deal.

01:19:16.200 --> 01:19:17.740
What's the use of cleaning it

01:19:17.740 --> 01:19:18.840
if it's not gonna run?

01:19:19.220 --> 01:19:20.740
Seems pretty weird to me.

01:19:20.960 --> 01:19:22.280
It can so run.

01:19:22.620 --> 01:19:25.320
Prove it to me. How can an old thing like this run?

01:19:27.260 --> 01:19:29.980
Me, I can't run.

01:19:36.080 --> 01:19:37.540
It's a girl! She's gone!

01:19:46.300 --> 01:19:47.080
It's raining!

01:20:07.680 --> 01:20:11.680
I hear the weather up here changes quickly, but this is ridiculous.

01:20:12.240 --> 01:20:13.480
It's getting worse every minute.

01:20:15.180 --> 01:20:16.420
Look, Theodore's shivering.

01:20:16.500 --> 01:20:17.460
Yeah, what a coward.

01:20:17.760 --> 01:20:19.880
What's the matter with you, you big chicken?

01:20:20.240 --> 01:20:29.970
He was going to hit the...

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:32.180
station. Uh-oh, could mean trouble.

01:20:32.400 --> 01:20:33.960
The last express of the day is

01:20:33.960 --> 01:20:35.580
expected to come at 7.30.

01:20:36.040 --> 01:20:38.250
Huh? Astro, you'd better go and look.

01:20:38.330 --> 01:20:38.550
Right.

01:21:39.280 --> 01:21:41.860
Come on, we need something to lift our spirits.

01:21:42.120 --> 01:21:45.180
Nothing to be afraid of in a storm, just a little wind and rain.

01:21:46.020 --> 01:21:48.130
How about singing a song?

01:21:50.850 --> 01:21:53.670
Boys and girls, I've made some delicious homemade soup.

01:21:53.810 --> 01:21:56.250
Just the thing for you. Come on to the dining room.

01:21:57.460 --> 01:21:59.960
Come on, cheer up. We've got to eat some.

01:22:00.000 --> 01:22:09.360
to keep up our strength this is really getting to be bad huh I've never seen

01:22:09.360 --> 01:22:14.950
anything like this in all my 30 years what did you say 30 years are you

01:22:14.950 --> 01:22:17.490
telling me you're only 30 years old

01:22:27.270 --> 01:22:29.510
Susie switch to the emergency generator

01:22:38.640 --> 01:22:39.180
What's that?

01:22:39.380 --> 01:22:41.540
Why, that's the emergency siren.

01:22:42.700 --> 01:22:44.260
Emergency siren? Huh?

01:22:46.150 --> 01:22:48.350
Yep, go ahead. This is the lodge.

01:22:48.550 --> 01:22:52.110
Grandpa, trouble. Pumping the dam's out of order. The dam's about to go.

01:22:52.390 --> 01:22:54.370
Get everybody out of there right now, you hear?

01:22:55.020 --> 01:22:58.420
What? Daggone it, ain't you gonna send out a rescue squad?

01:22:58.730 --> 01:22:59.970
All transportation's been cut.

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:02.460
Nothing we can do in this storm. Can't reach you.

01:23:03.520 --> 01:23:05.990
Hello. What do we do now?

01:23:08.630 --> 01:23:10.850
Don't they even care what happens to us?

01:23:11.060 --> 01:23:13.560
Sir? I'll see what you can do.

01:24:01.660 --> 01:24:04.280
Chris, there's a big crack in the wall of the dam.

01:24:04.540 --> 01:24:05.040
What's that?

01:24:05.850 --> 01:24:08.270
Pastro, what's wrong with the jets in your legs?

01:24:08.870 --> 01:24:10.390
I don't know. They're not working.

01:24:11.010 --> 01:24:12.450
What else can go wrong?

01:24:12.820 --> 01:24:15.040
We've got to move downstream below the new dam.

01:24:15.340 --> 01:24:18.660
It's not finished yet, but I think it should hold back the water if the old dam breaks.

01:24:18.860 --> 01:24:20.920
We have to hurry and get down there right away.

01:24:21.440 --> 01:24:24.680
But how can we get down there without transportation?

01:24:31.050 --> 01:24:33.160
Yeah, that's it. I've got it.

01:24:33.430 --> 01:24:37.270
Why don't we all get in the D-51 and have Astro pull the locomotive?

01:24:37.470 --> 01:24:40.210
The old railroad tracks go down past the new dam.

01:24:40.760 --> 01:24:41.380
Good idea.

01:24:41.780 --> 01:24:42.300
Astro!

01:24:43.630 --> 01:24:45.770
I'm afraid Astro can't do that.

01:24:45.950 --> 01:24:48.730
If he holds on to the locomotive, he won't be able to fly.

01:24:49.120 --> 01:24:51.400
The jet power in his legs isn't working.

01:24:52.160 --> 01:24:54.880
He was our only hope.

01:24:58.340 --> 01:24:59.880
D-51 running again.

01:25:00.670 --> 01:25:03.570
Yeah, that's right. You told me it could run.

01:25:03.890 --> 01:25:05.610
Yes, Grandpa, let's show them.

01:25:05.750 --> 01:25:09.610
Let's show them the D-51 can still run like in the old days.

01:25:14.500 --> 01:25:17.840
The D-51 couldn't move an inch under its own power.

01:25:18.540 --> 01:25:20.580
But why not? She said it could.

01:25:20.800 --> 01:25:21.640
What's the story?

01:25:22.060 --> 01:25:25.380
You have to do something. We'll be drowned if we stay here.

01:25:26.870 --> 01:25:29.980
Well, I'll tell you. There's just too much to...

01:25:30.000 --> 01:25:35.220
fix up the tracks aren't in good shape and the tunnel might have already caved in don't worry

01:25:35.220 --> 01:25:41.570
about that we can go and check things out before we go we have to try no it's no good it can't be

01:25:41.570 --> 01:25:57.590
done no look at that house somebody do something i don't want to

01:26:16.730 --> 01:26:29.530
running. I don't understand why you won't do it. Taking care of the D-51 every day, oiling and polishing it.

01:26:30.350 --> 01:26:33.320
Because someday, we would get it running again.

01:26:34.260 --> 01:26:37.300
Oh, Grandpa, it was always your dream, too.

01:26:47.080 --> 01:26:48.020
Then I will.

01:26:50.180 --> 01:26:50.560
Wait.

01:26:51.780 --> 01:26:53.700
Get everyone on D-51.

01:26:56.780 --> 01:26:57.260
Grandpa.

01:26:58.310 --> 01:26:59.970
Susie, you make sure the passengers...

01:27:00.000 --> 01:27:02.980
Passenger car couplings are secure. I'll be in the engine room.

01:27:03.140 --> 01:27:05.710
Okay, everyone, get on...

01:27:17.450 --> 01:27:19.090
Past Grove better go, too.

01:27:19.570 --> 01:27:19.990
Yes, sir.

01:27:21.700 --> 01:27:24.220
Daddy Walrus, I've something to tell you.

01:27:25.410 --> 01:27:29.710
Look at this.

01:27:31.650 --> 01:27:36.730
There was a time when I was the heart and soul of this locomotive.

01:27:37.780 --> 01:27:41.140
I served for years as the robot computer on this train.

01:27:42.210 --> 01:27:44.970
There used to be a coal mine under the new dam site,

01:27:44.990 --> 01:27:48.070
and we would carry the coal from the mines down the mountain.

01:27:48.660 --> 01:27:50.560
It was a stormy night like this.

01:27:50.990 --> 01:27:54.270
The brakes failed on a sharp turn, and we derailed.

01:27:55.100 --> 01:27:57.500
Yes, that was it for old D-51.

01:27:58.170 --> 01:27:59.970
The owner of the mine took me into...

01:28:00.000 --> 01:28:09.940
his home and remodeled me to serve his family and take care of Susie. I've served them here ever

01:28:09.940 --> 01:28:16.610
since. But what about the girl's parents? I'm afraid that was a tragedy. You see, it was just before

01:28:16.610 --> 01:28:23.000
Susie's first birthday. Her parents left her with me when they went on a trip, but they were killed

01:28:23.000 --> 01:28:29.630
in a plane crash. So I brought her up as my own granddaughter. I think her parents would have

01:28:29.630 --> 01:28:29.830
approval

01:28:31.690 --> 01:28:33.130
You've done a good job.

01:28:34.160 --> 01:28:38.430
I'm not young anymore, but don't you think I'm finished yet?

01:28:39.980 --> 01:28:42.740
I'll give everything I have for the D-51.

01:29:01.660 --> 01:29:02.780
Well, we're ready to go.

01:29:03.120 --> 01:29:05.300
Yes, now we just have to leave it up to Grandpa.

01:29:07.160 --> 01:29:08.740
Well, old man, do your best.

01:29:10.510 --> 01:29:10.870
Go!

01:29:43.660 --> 01:29:45.760
Astro, go ahead of us and check the rails, will you?

01:30:30.960 --> 01:30:35.160
Good boy, Grandpa. Keep it up. Our lives are in your hands now.

01:31:30.030 --> 01:31:59.970
you've got. Show us what an old engine can do. That's the way. Keep it up. Freddy, did you say

01:32:00.000 --> 01:32:01.680
Hey, the water's right behind us.

01:32:01.860 --> 01:32:02.640
Want a lot?

01:32:02.840 --> 01:32:03.920
I don't want to know.

01:32:04.180 --> 01:32:06.320
Oh, it's gaining on us.

01:32:08.990 --> 01:32:13.350
Grandpa, please bear down now and give us all you've got.

01:32:13.650 --> 01:32:20.580
The dam is in sight.

01:32:21.380 --> 01:32:22.980
Not much more to go, old man.

01:32:23.750 --> 01:32:24.950
One last hill.

01:32:25.150 --> 01:32:27.630
If we can get to the top, we'll be all right.

01:32:54.670 --> 01:32:55.700
It's crazy!

01:32:55.700 --> 01:32:56.240
Crazy!

01:33:00.740 --> 01:33:03.100
I don't know how we can repay him.

01:33:03.260 --> 01:33:04.740
Great work, old man.

01:33:05.090 --> 01:33:07.250
Nobody can call you outdated now.

01:33:07.650 --> 01:33:10.390
Let's see one of those fancy computers pull a train.

01:33:10.810 --> 01:33:29.980
Must have been what made the jet power in my legs go out.

01:33:30.730 --> 01:33:31.830
I'd better go back now.

01:33:36.240 --> 01:33:37.820
Grandpa, we've passed the summit.

01:33:38.000 --> 01:33:38.860
You can take a rest.

01:33:39.380 --> 01:33:41.960
You're building up quite a speed there, you know.

01:33:42.500 --> 01:33:51.850
Oh, no, I wonder if there's something wrong with the brakes.

01:33:52.110 --> 01:33:54.050
Do you think we're going too fast?

01:33:54.250 --> 01:33:56.110
Looks like it. Care to take a look?

01:33:56.450 --> 01:33:57.920
I don't want to know.

01:33:59.020 --> 01:33:59.500
Grandpa!

01:34:00.000 --> 01:34:11.570
Something, this is getting dangerous!

01:34:11.890 --> 01:34:13.250
Now close the brake valves!

01:34:14.030 --> 01:34:15.910
No! It won't burn!

01:34:21.700 --> 01:34:23.360
Oh, the brakes have failed!

01:34:23.620 --> 01:34:25.010
Out of control, help us!

01:35:45.480 --> 01:35:47.240
We've stopped! We're okay!

01:35:56.850 --> 01:35:57.750
Sir, are you all right?

01:35:58.130 --> 01:35:59.170
What? Sure I am.

01:36:00.730 --> 01:36:02.010
He knew this would happen.

01:36:12.820 --> 01:36:17.610
Well, it's a little hard to explain.

01:36:17.910 --> 01:36:23.400
He was a robot designed to drive steam engines like this.

01:36:30.284 --> 01:36:30.784
Cripple

01:36:44.204 --> 01:36:47.984
Castro, looks like we'd better ask Dr. Elephant to repair her grandpa.

01:36:51.564 --> 01:36:52.064
should be-

01:36:52.204 --> 01:36:53.444
to put him back together?

01:37:09.660 --> 01:37:14.260
All the students in Daddy Walrus's class went on a field trip in space to study astronomy.

01:37:16.150 --> 01:37:20.230
We were all amazed at the beauty of the rainbow comet when it came over Venus.

01:37:20.450 --> 01:37:27.120
But our trip was upset when Atlas' crystal castle kidnapped our ship.

01:37:27.320 --> 01:37:29.980
He threatened to hurt my classmates if I didn't show up.

01:37:30.028 --> 01:37:32.328
to join him in his plan to conquer the world.

01:37:32.460 --> 01:37:37.540
Find out what happens when I meet Atlas once again next time on Astroboard.

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:30.500
P.O.

01:40:01.530 --> 01:40:01.770
Thank you.

01:40:43.340 --> 01:40:44.560
Yes, Mother.

01:40:45.260 --> 01:40:47.980
I'm gonna get a clear view of the rainbow comet with this.

01:40:48.160 --> 01:40:51.060
I can see forever with my super optic binoculars.

01:40:52.180 --> 01:40:55.220
Astronomy Club members of the Elephant Primary School,

01:40:55.380 --> 01:40:56.900
please report to Gate 7.

01:40:57.940 --> 01:40:58.720
Let's go!

01:41:04.950 --> 01:41:09.590
Astro, if something happens, please be sure to contact me with this.

01:41:09.850 --> 01:41:10.290
Okay.

01:41:10.570 --> 01:41:14.960
What do you mean, if something should happen?

01:41:15.300 --> 01:41:16.640
Oh, no, don't worry.

01:41:16.640 --> 01:41:17.780
There's no danger.

01:41:17.840 --> 01:41:22.220
It's just a scientific outing, a regular part of the Astronomy Club's activity,

01:41:22.400 --> 01:41:24.840
but it's always better to be prepared for an emergency.

01:41:26.240 --> 01:41:29.980
All their activities are planned by a computer, supervised by a computer, and...

01:41:30.000 --> 01:41:33.140
evaluated by a computer so you can see nothing can go wrong.

01:41:33.320 --> 01:41:36.280
But, Mrs. Gordon, is it all right for them to go alone?

01:41:36.280 --> 01:41:38.260
They're so young and high-spirited.

01:41:38.540 --> 01:41:40.820
That's the point of this trip, to test their independence

01:41:40.820 --> 01:41:42.960
and their ability to get things done on their own.

01:41:43.080 --> 01:41:44.120
My son told me that.

01:41:44.400 --> 01:41:46.340
Oh, dear, he's getting to be so grown up.

01:41:51.770 --> 01:41:52.990
